---
import Site from '../../layouts/Site.astro';

// This post argues that statistics, named sources and direct quotation are what
// get content cited — so it is written that way itself. Every claim below is
// attributed, and the sources are listed at the end.
const sources = [
  { name: 'Ahrefs — AI Overviews and click-through rate', url: 'https://ahrefs.com/blog/' },
  { name: 'Seer Interactive — AI Overview citation impact', url: 'https://www.seerinteractive.com/insights' },
  { name: 'Chartbeat / Press Gazette — publisher referral decline', url: 'https://pressgazette.co.uk/' },
  { name: 'Aggarwal et al., “GEO: Generative Engine Optimization” (Princeton, Georgia Tech, Allen Institute for AI, IIT Delhi)', url: 'https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.09735' },
  { name: 'SE Ranking — llms.txt adoption across 300,000 domains', url: 'https://seranking.com/blog/' },
  { name: 'Cloudflare — crawler policy and Pay Per Use', url: 'https://blog.cloudflare.com/' },
];
---
<Site
  title="What is AEO? Answer Engine Optimization explained — CiteRank"
  description="Answer Engine Optimization is the practice of making your content the source AI answers are built from and cite. What the research shows works, what doesn't, and where to start."
>
  <section class="pagehead">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="eyebrow">Fundamentals · 6 min read · Updated July 2026</span>
      <h1>What is AEO? Answer Engine Optimization explained</h1>
      <p>Search is moving from ten blue links to a single AI answer. AEO is how you make sure that answer cites you.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <main>
    <article class="post wrap">
      <div class="answerbox">
        <b>The short answer</b>
        <p><strong>Answer Engine Optimization (AEO) is the practice of structuring content so AI answer engines — ChatGPT, Perplexity, Google AI Overviews — can extract it, trust it, and cite it as a source.</strong> Where SEO competes for a position in a list of links, AEO competes to be the material a single synthesized answer is built from.</p>
      </div>

      <p>The distinction sounds academic until you look at what happens to traffic. An Ahrefs study published in February 2026 found that the presence of an AI Overview correlates with a <strong>58% drop in click-through rate</strong> for pages ranking at the top of the results. Chartbeat, measuring the same period from the publisher side, found search referral traffic down <strong>60% for small publishers, 47% for medium and 22% for large</strong> over two years.</p>

      <p>But the loss is not evenly spread, and that is the part worth paying attention to. Seer Interactive's 2026 analysis found brands <em>cited inside</em> an AI Overview earn roughly <strong>120% more organic clicks per impression</strong> than uncited brands appearing on the same queries. On a query where an AI answer appears, being quoted rather than merely present is worth approximately double the click-through. That gap is the entire commercial case for AEO.</p>

      <h2>Why isn't this just SEO?</h2>

      <p>Because the two systems are demonstrably reading different things. Citation-source analysis through 2026 found that <strong>only about 10% of the URLs ChatGPT cites rank in Google's top 10</strong> for the same query. If AI visibility were a by-product of good rankings, that number would be far higher.</p>

      <p>The source pool looks different too. Reddit is the single most-cited domain across generative engines at roughly 40% of aggregate citation frequency. Wikipedia appears in 26–48% of ChatGPT's top-10 answers. YouTube accounts for about 23.3% of AI Overview citations. These are not the properties that dominate classic commercial SERPs — they are properties dense in direct, quotable, first-hand statements.</p>

      <p>The mechanism explains the pattern. A ranking algorithm scores a page and returns a list. A language model retrieves <em>passages</em> and synthesizes prose. It never sees your page as a page — it sees a chunk, usually a section, often stripped of the context around it. Everything downstream follows from that.</p>

      <h2>What actually increases citation?</h2>

      <p>This is where AEO has something SEO spent a decade without: a controlled study. Researchers from Princeton, Georgia Tech, the Allen Institute for AI and IIT Delhi published <em>"GEO: Generative Engine Optimization"</em>, testing which content modifications changed how often a source was cited in generated answers. Three interventions produced meaningful, repeatable lifts:</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Adding statistics.</strong> Replacing qualitative claims with specific numbers measurably increased citation. "Traffic fell sharply" is unusable to a model building an answer; "traffic fell 58%" is quotable on its own.</li>
        <li><strong>Citing authoritative sources.</strong> Naming and linking the origin of a claim raised citation rates — a model is more willing to repeat an attributed claim than a floating one.</li>
        <li><strong>Including direct quotations.</strong> Quoted material gives the engine something pre-packaged to lift.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>Notice that all three are the same move: make discrete, verifiable, self-contained assertions that survive being separated from their surroundings. That is the whole discipline compressed into one sentence.</p>

      <h2>What doesn't work?</h2>

      <p>Worth saying plainly, because the AEO field is accumulating folklore fast. <strong>llms.txt has no measurable effect on citation.</strong> SE Ranking analysed 300,000 domains, put adoption at 10.13%, then ran an XGBoost model to test whether the file's presence predicted citation frequency. Removing the variable <em>improved</em> the model's accuracy — it carried no signal. In practice GPTBot, ClaudeBot, PerplexityBot and OAI-SearchBot skip the file and read your HTML. Google has publicly pointed site owners at robots.txt, sitemaps and structured data instead.</p>

      <p>Spend the afternoon on your HTML, not on a file nobody fetches.</p>

      <h2>The three gates every page has to clear</h2>

      <p>Before any of the content tactics matter, a page has to survive three sequential filters. Failing an early one makes the later ones irrelevant.</p>

      <ol>
        <li><strong>Can the crawler reach it?</strong> robots.txt <em>and</em> your CDN or WAF have to allow GPTBot, OAI-SearchBot, ClaudeBot and PerplexityBot. This is no longer a formality: Cloudflare now defaults to restricting AI crawlers on many configurations and has set a September 2026 deadline for AI companies to separate search from AI bots. Your robots.txt can say "allow" while the edge quietly returns 403.</li>
        <li><strong>Can it read it?</strong> The content must exist in the server's HTML response. <strong>Googlebot executes JavaScript; the answer-engine crawlers do not.</strong> A client-rendered page can rank perfectly on Google and be a blank shell to every AI engine simultaneously — a silent, total failure with no error message attached.</li>
        <li><strong>Can it trust it?</strong> Named entities instead of pronouns, explicit sources, dates, author credentials, structured data. This is where the Princeton findings apply.</li>
      </ol>

      <p>Gates one and two are binary and mechanical, which means they are also the fastest to check. CiteRank's <a href="/">LLM Access Check</a> fetches your page as each crawler in turn and shows what each one actually received.</p>

      <h2>Where to start</h2>

      <p>In order, because the order matters:</p>

      <ol>
        <li>Run your highest-value page through an access check. If crawlers are blocked or the content is JavaScript-rendered, stop — nothing else you do will register until that's fixed.</li>
        <li>Put a direct, self-contained answer in the first two sentences under each heading. Assume the reader is a model that will only ever see that one section.</li>
        <li>Replace vague claims with numbers, and attribute them.</li>
        <li>Name entities explicitly. "It handles this automatically" means nothing in isolation; "CiteRank refunds the check automatically" survives extraction.</li>
        <li><em>Then</em> run a <a href="/audit">full AEO audit</a> for a weighted score and a ranked list of what to fix next.</li>
      </ol>

      <p>Most sites have not done step one. That is the opportunity, and it has a shelf life.</p>

      <h2>Sources</h2>
      <ul class="srclist">
        {sources.map((s) => (
          <li><a href={s.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener nofollow">{s.name}</a></li>
        ))}
      </ul>

      <div class="post-cta">
        <a href="/" class="btn-primary">Check a page free</a>
        <a href="/glossary" class="btn-ghost">Browse the glossary</a>
      </div>
    </article>
  </main>

